$DATUM 7732-18-5
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Synthesis of n-Hexyl Chloride under Continuous Flow Conditions (Table 2).
Five milliliters of 30% aqueous HCl were loaded into the PTFE loop module
(Figure 3). By pumping water (flow rate feed A: 47.9 μL/min) into the
loop the conc. aq HCl was driven into the microreactor, while feed B
(n-hexanol flow rate 18.8 μL/min) was directly pumped into the two-feed
glass microreactor. The 1 mL glass microreactor chip was heated to 180 °C
in the chip heater, and the reaction mixture was pumped through the
reactor (overall flow rate 66.67 μL/min) and left the microreactor after
15 min of reaction time by passing through a dynamic backpressure
regulator (pmax set to 18-19 bar) into the collection vial. After 30 min
a total volume of 4 mL (1.128 mL of 1-hexanol and 2.874 mL of 30% HCl)
was processed. n-Hexyl chloride was isolated by phase separation from the
aqueous phase and dried in a desiccator over CaCl2(s). Yield, 927 mg,
85%. >95% purity. 1H NMR (CDCl3): δ = 0.89-0.93 (t, J= 12 Hz, 3H),
1.32-1.46 (m, 6H), 1.74-1.83 (m, 2H), 3.53-3.57 (t, J=- 15 Hz, 2H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM green chemistry-process simplification, high pressure, thermal, other
product also detected, glass microreactor chip used, optimized on
reaction conditions (temperature and various microwave batch conditions)
concerning an environment friendly and scalable synthesis method,
alternative reaction conditions shown (microwave batch) gave similar
yield and were used for kinetic and optimization studies, scalable,
optimization study, flow system used (continuous), 15m residence time
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Continuous Flow Synthesis of n-Alkyl Chlorides in a High-Temperature
Microreactor Environment
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Reichart, Benedikt; Tekautz, Guenter; Kappe, C. Oliver
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Process Research & Development (2013), 17(1), 152-157

