$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General procedure for catalytic chlorination and bromination of alcohol
(Table 3) A solution of oxalyl chloride (1.1 mmol) in dichloromethane (5
mL) was added via syringe pump over the indicated time to a stirring
solution of the alcohol substrate (1.0 mmol) and tropone (indicated mol%)
in dichloromethane (5 mL) at rt (75 °C in chloroform for entry 4). After
the addition is completed, the reaction mixture was stirred at that
temperature for another 1 h. When the reaction was complete (monitored by
TLC or 1H NMR), the reaction mixture was concentrated under reduced
pressure and then purified by column chromatography (SiO2, hexanes ->
dichloromethane:hexanes 1:9) to obtain the chloride or bromide product.
(Chloromethyl)benzene 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 7.45-7.30 (m, 5H), 4.60
(s, 2H) ppm. 13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3) δ 137.7, 128.9, 128.8, 128.6, 46.5
ppm.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM alternative method stoichiometric tropone, in-situ generated catalyst
(1,1-dichlorotropone)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Aromatic Cation Activation: Nucleophilic Substitution of Alcohols and
Carboxylic Acids
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Nguyen, Thanh V.; Bekensir, Alp
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2014), 16(6), 1720-1723

